WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.603 --> 00:00:02.817
Hi there!
Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:08.278
I haven't done a video in a long time.
I feel a bit guilty, so I've decided to do one.

00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:14.648
I've got my cross-country
skiing shirt on here.

00:00:15.164 --> 00:00:17.870
It's been warm, but I went
in for a swim in the ocean

00:00:17.886 --> 00:00:20.815
and it was pretty
chilly in there.

00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:25.497
Bracing, maybe 17
degrees Celsius.

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:29.100
I'm not sure quite how cool, but
it sure cools you off, so...

00:00:29.844 --> 00:00:34.970
Sitting here in the evening I've got
my extra little sweatshirt over my t-shirt.

00:00:35.454 --> 00:00:39.002
Today, I want to talk about
efficiency in language learning.

00:00:40.682 --> 00:00:42.666
And... Because I've been
thinking about what,

00:00:42.674 --> 00:00:45.388
you know, my learning
activities here in Czech.

00:00:46.582 --> 00:00:49.037
I started Czech
about 5 weeks ago.

00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:55.001
I've been working primarily at LingQ.
I did find some material at home.

00:00:55.302 --> 00:00:57.770
An old phrase book
which I listen to.

00:00:58.993 --> 00:01:02.533
You know, I find once you get going in
the language you tend to .. buy things.

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:04.801
I was in a bookstore,
I saw this small

00:01:05.018 --> 00:01:09.963
book on learning Czech - I don't have
it around here - but it was actually

00:01:10.181 --> 00:01:12.919
from a group called the
"Hippocrene Books",

00:01:12.935 --> 00:01:15.252
it's "Beginner's Czech
- with 2 Audio CDs".

00:01:15.712 --> 00:01:17.054
So, I went through that.

00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:20.926
And...
So I had an interesting sort of...

00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:23.958
You know, observation.

00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:29.759
On the one hand, if I listen to the
easy content in this beginner's Czech,

00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:33.217
and they read the text
once at normal speed,

00:01:33.241 --> 00:01:34.971
and they read it
very, very slowly

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:38.154
and then they record all the
vocabulary, you can listen to that.

00:01:38.471 --> 00:01:40.582
And, of course, the first
time you listen to it,

00:01:41.304 --> 00:01:45.328
it's wonderful to be able to understand
everything, because most of my listening,

00:01:47.347 --> 00:01:49.716
I guess it's been
divided into two types:

00:01:49.781 --> 00:01:53.470
I have the kind of material that has
been created by our members at LingQ,

00:01:54.227 --> 00:01:58.146
most of which is, I would say, half of which
is easy and half of which is not easy;

00:01:59.011 --> 00:02:02.542
and then I've been listening
to radio Praha - "Rádio Praha";

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.485
and I've been listening to this
audiobook "The Good Soldier Švejk".

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:12.696
And, of course, the radio Praha is difficult,
"The Good Soldier Švejk" is also very difficult.

00:02:12.916 --> 00:02:18.037
But I have text for that, so I
read it on LingQ, I save my linQs.

00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:19.983
I can review
them on my iPad,

00:02:19.993 --> 00:02:25.946
I can sit down and go over them in iLingQ many,
many times and then I listen when go running.

00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:29.727
So, all of this is just kind of letting
all the language wash over me,

00:02:30.076 --> 00:02:33.947
occasionally reviewing
them in flashcards, so...

00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:35.346
And I've been
doing mostly that.

00:02:35.958 --> 00:02:42.100
And plus, also reading this Czech
newspaper, which I have online here.

00:02:42.118 --> 00:02:44.467
Which I go to
everyday and read.

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:48.356
I use the lingQ bookmarklet,
and bring it in.

00:02:48.496 --> 00:02:52.918
This is "ČeskéNoviny"

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.028
And so, here
is an article:

00:02:55.241 --> 00:03:03.434
"Pařížská konference žádá odblokování
zmrazených libyjských fondů".

00:03:03.873 --> 00:03:05.103
Yeah, I understand that all.

00:03:05.111 --> 00:03:07.626
And I read the article and there's
words that I don't understand

00:03:07.642 --> 00:03:11.388
but they want to unblock the
frozen funds and so forth.

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:12.617
But...

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.052
And that is just
text that I read,

00:03:14.052 --> 00:03:20.243
 but I find now that I'm able to read these
newspaper articles, you know, not too bad.

00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:24.053
You know, my text in LingQ
is covered in yellow

00:03:24.124 --> 00:03:26.021
highlighted words, because these
are the words that I've looked up

00:03:26.037 --> 00:03:27.707
and I've seen again
and again and again

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:29.866
and they gradually
starting to stick.

00:03:30.815 --> 00:03:34.204
But in "Rádio Praha" when I
listen to those articles,

00:03:34.268 --> 00:03:36.744
even though I read them and
save the words and study them

00:03:36.768 --> 00:03:39.927
when I hear it, you know,
I understand 30-40%.

00:03:40.165 --> 00:03:43.085
So, when I got
back to this

00:03:43.125 --> 00:03:45.513
"Czech for beginners" -
whatever it was here -

00:03:45.562 --> 00:03:48.609
with 2 CDs, the
"Hippocrene Books",

00:03:48.776 --> 00:03:50.673
of course, I understood
just about everything.

00:03:50.808 --> 00:03:53.061
But there's still a few words
that I didn't understand.

00:03:53.077 --> 00:03:54.879
And then I would
read the text.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.918
And I: "Ahh, now I understand.
Yeah, it's that word,

00:03:57.941 --> 00:03:59.615
I didn't quite make it
out when I was listening

00:03:59.623 --> 00:04:01.592
and now I listen again
and I still listen.

00:04:02.527 --> 00:04:04.306
So, then I started
saying to myself:

00:04:04.352 --> 00:04:06.796
Am I better of to
spend a lot of time

00:04:06.804 --> 00:04:10.367
trying to nail down
everything in this 10 lessons,

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:11.741
in this beginner book?

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.653
Or, am I better
of to continue

00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:17.042
listening "Rádio Praha",
reading the transcripts,

00:04:17.074 --> 00:04:18.860
saving the words
and phrases,

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:24.455
going of to the Czech
newspaper "ČeskéNoviny",

00:04:24.694 --> 00:04:27.138
and reading more articles
about Václav Klaus

00:04:27.146 --> 00:04:30.075
and his latest outrageous
statements or whatever?

00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:36.892
And, doing a bit of "Švejk" and
just letting the words wash over me.

00:04:37.241 --> 00:04:39.083
Because on the one
hand, doing that

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:42.534
has seen my vocabulary
really, really increase.

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.462
Where I... It's...
I feel that I've done...

00:04:44.495 --> 00:04:48.153
I've achieved greater
progress in Czech in 5 weeks,

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:50.486
than I can't remember
in any other language.

00:04:50.565 --> 00:04:54.053
Now, it helps that I did Russian,
the structure is very similar.

00:04:54.192 --> 00:04:57.049
The vocabulary is
surprisingly, you know,

00:04:57.057 --> 00:05:01.437
50% Russian, or similar to
Russian, 50% quite different.

00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:05.259
So, and then in the end, I ended
up saying to myself, you know,

00:05:05.275 --> 00:05:09.286
it's very satisfying to
try to nail stuff down.

00:05:09.528 --> 00:05:13.512
Listen to this... this video here...
or this video...

00:05:13.512 --> 00:05:15.443
This audio CD here.

00:05:15.543 --> 00:05:18.638
And listen and listen and listen,
until I nail down every word,

00:05:18.665 --> 00:05:21.871
and I nail down every expression
and I listen to it over and over.

00:05:23.301 --> 00:05:25.754
But at the end of that I
haven't learned many words.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.531
Because I know most of
the words in this book.

00:05:28.563 --> 00:05:30.913
This whole beginner book,
basically I'm there.

00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:32.793
You know, there aren't
many words that I don't know,

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:36.102
 even though I don't necessarily pick
them up every time when I listen.

00:05:37.053 --> 00:05:40.292
So I, you know, I kind of think
you need to do a bit of both.

00:05:40.784 --> 00:05:43.955
So, I don't mind doing a bit
of the simple whatever.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.804
Listening to it a few times, it helps
pick up on some pronunciation issues,

00:05:47.867 --> 00:05:49.939
it helps on some
structure issues.

00:05:50.271 --> 00:05:52.771
But I'm inclined to spend
more and more of my time

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:56.691
simply exposing myself to the
language through interesting content.

00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:00.438
LingQing it, seeing the number
of yellow words increase,

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.075
and of course, in LingQ the
yellow it's a dark yellow

00:06:03.102 --> 00:06:04.553
and as you've known
a little better

00:06:04.571 --> 00:06:07.058
it becomes a lighter yellow
and a lighter yellow still.

00:06:07.781 --> 00:06:11.088
I think that process is
bringing me more words.

00:06:11.111 --> 00:06:14.083
And it's bringing me more
familiarity with the language.

00:06:14.758 --> 00:06:15.883
And so...
And then...

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.880
I remember when I
studied Chinese,

00:06:17.907 --> 00:06:19.926
I basically convinced
myself that

00:06:19.931 --> 00:06:22.471
the most important thing in
language learning was efficiency.

00:06:23.137 --> 00:06:25.060
Because efficiency
means intensity.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.539
The more efficiently you learn, the
more intense your learning is.

00:06:28.659 --> 00:06:32.403
And I felt that 40 years ago when
I was studying Chinese that...

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:37.029
And I did my Chinese... Did the "British
Foreign Service Exam" whatever in...

00:06:37.047 --> 00:06:40.535
After 9 months of studying.
The other people took 2 years.

00:06:40.637 --> 00:06:43.314
But I did it, you know,
completely like a fanatic.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.488
I was at it 5, 6,
7, 8 hours a day.

00:06:45.665 --> 00:06:47.896
And I felt that I
learned it better.

00:06:48.113 --> 00:06:50.379
Because I did it so
much more intensely

00:06:50.613 --> 00:06:53.445
than these other students who
were taking their time at it.

00:06:53.758 --> 00:06:56.505
And so, I got hung up, really
started to think about efficiency.

00:06:56.523 --> 00:06:59.231
I developed my efficient
way of learning characters.

00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:02.459
And what I thought was an efficient
way of learning the language,

00:07:02.508 --> 00:07:04.119
which was to ignore the
grammar explanations,

00:07:04.127 --> 00:07:08.428
which was to notice the patterns
and get enough content in me

00:07:08.444 --> 00:07:10.586
so that these patterns
start to become natural.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.222
This was my way of becoming
efficient in Chinese.

00:07:13.793 --> 00:07:15.586
So, talking about
efficiency.

00:07:17.126 --> 00:07:18.959
When I think of what...
how I did it. What I had to do

00:07:18.975 --> 00:07:22.213
when I started with Russian
at LingQ, 3-4 years ago.

00:07:22.483 --> 00:07:25.348
It was so slow and clunky and...

00:07:25.561 --> 00:07:28.957
At times it took 7, 8, 10
seconds to save, you know,

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.497
go to the dictionary, get
the meaning, bring it back.

00:07:31.512 --> 00:07:33.965
And now it's all there
with a mouse over.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.766
Like, LingQ itself has become
so much more efficient.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.456
And, so therefore in the
older less efficient LingQ

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:43.289
I did relatively more
listening, because

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.701
A- it's easier to do,
it's more pleasant;

00:07:45.852 --> 00:07:49.185
because it was a bit of a chore
to save lingQs in LingQ.

00:07:49.455 --> 00:07:53.193
Whereas now, I find myself doing
more and more reading in LingQ.

00:07:53.471 --> 00:07:55.876
Because I'm able to cover
interesting content,

00:07:56.265 --> 00:07:59.542
it's the challenge of reading
something new and interesting.

00:07:59.678 --> 00:08:03.368
I still listen when I go running,
I listen when I'm in my car.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.495
But I'm doing relatively
more reading.

00:08:06.535 --> 00:08:07.098
So...

00:08:07.273 --> 00:08:08.360
I mean, everybody...

00:08:08.376 --> 00:08:11.693
It's going to depend on what
you like, what you like to do.

00:08:11.717 --> 00:08:16.756
But I find it quite efficient
to go after new content.

00:08:17.929 --> 00:08:20.364
Especially if I'm familiar
with the context,

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:24.049
because it's almost easier to
understand something that talks about,

00:08:24.068 --> 00:08:28.390
I don't know, Libya and the
meeting in Paris about Libya,

00:08:28.465 --> 00:08:31.118
when you understand the
context, than to read some

00:08:31.184 --> 00:08:34.982
artificial content that has been
written about so and so visiting

00:08:35.037 --> 00:08:38.186
their new found friends in Prague
and what they are having for dinner

00:08:38.231 --> 00:08:41.200
and all kinds of...
The names of the Czech dishes and stuff

00:08:41.223 --> 00:08:44.622
which is too early for me. I'll worry
about that when I get to Prague

00:08:44.644 --> 00:08:46.345
and I want to order
something in a restaurant.

00:08:46.921 --> 00:08:51.828
So, I think it's more efficient to,
again, to focus on content of interest,

00:08:51.873 --> 00:08:55.702
meaningful content and to push yourself
to acquire more and more words.

00:08:55.736 --> 00:08:59.180
And to allow this words and
phrases and structures

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.059
to become natural through
a lot of exposure.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:06.619
With LingQ, maybe sliding a bit more towards
the reading that I have done in the past.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:10.253
And then occasionally to go
back to something very simple

00:09:10.264 --> 00:09:13.980
to kind on get some grounding
in some very basic structure,

00:09:14.049 --> 00:09:16.473
very basic vocabulary
that you might have

00:09:16.495 --> 00:09:21.424
maybe not, you know...
Really, you know,

00:09:21.436 --> 00:09:25.832
learned it properly as you could have
on the first or second pass over, so...

00:09:26.003 --> 00:09:29.039
It's just a general rant
here on efficiency and

00:09:29.164 --> 00:09:32.769
also to update you on
my Czech learning.

00:09:33.119 --> 00:09:36.248
I'm going to continue my
input activities in Czech.

00:09:36.391 --> 00:09:39.217
And perhaps in a month or
so, maybe start talking

00:09:39.225 --> 00:09:42.187
and see where I end
up after 3 months.

00:09:42.198 --> 00:09:45.407
See if I'm able to hold up
my end of the conversation

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:47.573
in a few months from now.

00:09:47.608 --> 00:09:49.725
So, thank you for listening.

00:09:49.776 --> 00:09:50.811
Bye for now.

